<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>1.15.5: Practice</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m00337</md:content-id>
    <md:title>1.15.5: Practice</md:title>
    <md:uuid>2a915bb9-a73b-498e-ab78-d42012ecdef9</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section class="practice" id="sect-00001">
      <para id="para-00001">Complete the following questions to practice the skills you have learned in this lesson.</para>
      <exercise id="_87ea4671-e924-4106-8958-0fb20fd70e4b" class="multiple-choice">
        <problem id="prob-00001">
          <para id="para-00002">If y varies directly as \(x\) and \(y=14\), when \(x=3\). Find the equation that relates \(x\) and \(y\).</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00001">
          <para id="para-00003">Correct! \(y=\frac{14}3x\)</para>
          <para id="para-00004">Try again. Revisit activity 1.15.1.</para>
          <para id="para-00005">The correct answer is \(y=\frac{14}3x\).</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise id="e801f482-f79e-445c-a137-4b9b16a764c9" class="multiple-choice">
        <problem id="prob-00002">
          <para id="para-00006">The price, \(P\), that Eric pays for gas varies directly with the number of gallons, \(g\), he buys. It costs him $50 to buy 20 gallons of gas. Choose the equation that relates \(P\) and \(g\).</para>
          <para id="para-00007">Multiple Choice:</para>
          <para id="para-00008">\(P = 25g\)</para>
          <para id="para-00009">\(P=\frac{2.5}g\)</para>
          <para id="para-00010">\(G=\frac{2.5}p\)</para>
          <para id="para-00011">\(P=\frac{2.5}g\)</para>
          <para id="para-00012">\(P=\frac g{2.5}\)</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00002">
          <para id="para-00013">Correct! \(P = 2.5g\)</para>
          <para id="para-00014">Try again. Revisit activities 1.15.2 and 1.15.3.</para>
          <para id="para-00015">The correct answer is \(P = 2.5g\).</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise id="db54efcd-a1a1-4135-83cc-fd19b4ae9897" class="multiple-choice">
        <problem id="prob-00003">
          <para id="para-00016">Joseph is traveling on a road trip. The distance, \(d\), he travels before stopping for lunch varies directly with the speed, \(v\), he travels. He can travel 120 miles at a speed of 60 mph. How many miles would he travel before stopping for lunch at a rate of 65 mph?</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00003">
          <para id="para-00017">Correct! 130 miles</para>
          <para id="para-00018">Try again. Revisit activities 1.15.2 and 1.15.3.</para>
          <para id="para-00019">The correct answer is 130 miles.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise id="c88ca437-735d-4652-8b7a-c31bdc723a57" class="multiple-choice">
        <problem id="prob-00004">
          <para id="para-00020">The mass of a liquid, \(M\), varies directly with its volume, \(V\). A liquid with mass 16 kilograms has a volume of 2 liters. What is the volume, in liters, of this liquid if its mass is 128 kilograms?</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00004">
          <para id="para-00021">Correct! 16 liters. \(M=8V\)</para>
          <para id="para-00022">Try again. Revisit activities 1.15.2 and 1.15.3.</para>
          <para id="para-00023">The correct answer is 16 liters. \(M=8V\).</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise id="_968a0e07-07a0-4dfb-ab3f-a99e74be221f" class="multiple-choice">
        <problem id="prob-00005">
          <para id="para-00024">The length, \(L\), that a spring stretches varies directly with a weight, \(W\), placed at the end of the spring. When Sarah placed a 10-pound watermelon on a hanging scale, the spring stretched 5 inches. What weight of watermelon, in pounds, would stretch the spring 6 inches?</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00005">
          <para id="para-00025">Correct! 12 pounds, \(L=1/2 W\).</para>
          <para id="para-00026">Try again. Revisit activities 1.15.2 and 1.15.3.</para>
          <para id="para-00027">The correct answer is 12 pounds, \(L=\frac12W\).</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise id="f84ffff3-68b7-4dea-9903-7a1ca6ab2545" class="multiple-choice">
        <problem id="prob-00006">
          <para id="para-00028">The maximum load a beam, \( M\), will support varies directly with the square of the diagonal of the beam’s cross-section, \(d^2\). A beam with diagonal 6 inch will support a maximum load of 108 pounds. What load, in pounds, will a beam with a 10-inch diagonal support?</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00006">
          <para id="para-00029">Correct! 300 pounds. Use the direct variation equation, \(M=3d^2\).</para>
          <para id="para-00030">Try again. Revisit activities 1.15.2 and 1.15.3.</para>
          <para id="para-00031">The correct answer is 300 pounds. Use the direct variation equation, \(M=3d^2\).</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise id="_0ff5da75-2446-49a8-bd92-332f581059a2" class="multiple-choice">
        <problem id="prob-00007">
          <para id="para-00032">The number of calories, \(c\), burned varies directly with the amount of time, \(t\), spent exercising. Arnold burned 312 calories in 65 minutes exercising. How many calories would he burn if he exercises for 90 minutes?</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00007">
          <para id="para-00033">Correct! He would burn 432 calories.</para>
          <para id="para-00034">Try again. Revisit activities 1.15.2 and 1.15.3.</para>
          <para id="para-00035">The correct answer is is a. \(M\)\(=8V\); b. 16 liters.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
    </section>
  </content>
